// Formative practice quiz for components-and-jsx. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/components-and-jsx

::components-and-jsx o1 - capital letter::You define a function named banner that returns JSX and use it as a lowercase banner tag. What does React do?{
~Calls your function, because the names match exactly#Not quite. Matching names are not enough. Lowercase tags are always treated as HTML elements.
=Treats the tag as an unknown HTML element instead of calling your function#Correct. A lowercase tag name means an HTML tag, so component names start with a capital letter.
~Stops the build with a naming error#Not quite. The build succeeds; the mistake only shows on screen because your function never runs.
~Capitalizes the tag for you at run time#Not quite. React never rewrites your tag names.
}

::components-and-jsx o1 - reuse::A page needs the same price tag in five places. What is the React way to do this?{
~Copy the JSX five times so each can change separately#Not quite. Copies drift apart, and every fix must be made five times.
~Write five functions, one for each place#Not quite. Five functions repeat the same markup; one function can be used many times.
=Write one component and use its tag five times#Correct. One definition used many times is the reason components exist.
~Call the function once and copy the DOM it produces#Not quite. You describe the screen with tags and let React handle the DOM.
}

::components-and-jsx o2 - adjacent roots::A component returns an h2 and a paragraph side by side with nothing around them, and the compiler complains. What is the smallest correct fix that adds no element to the page?{
~Return them as two return statements#Not quite. A function stops at its first return, so the second never runs.
=Wrap them in a fragment#Correct. A fragment gives one root and adds nothing to the DOM.
~Wrap them in a div#Not quite. A div does fix the error, but it adds an element, and the question asked for none.
~Put a comma between them#Not quite. A comma does not make one root. JSX still needs one parent.
}

::components-and-jsx o2 - attribute names::Which attribute pair is correct JSX for a styled label tied to an input?{
=className and htmlFor#Correct. JSX uses JavaScript-style names because class and for are reserved words.
~class and for#Not quite. Those are the HTML names. JSX uses className and htmlFor.
~className and for#Not quite. className is right, but for must become htmlFor as well.
~cssClass and labelFor#Not quite. These names do not exist in React.
}

::components-and-jsx o3 - expression in braces::A component has const qty equal to 3 and a paragraph containing the text qty * 2 with no braces. What does the paragraph show?{
~The number 6#Not quite. That would need braces around the expression.
~Nothing, because the text is ignored#Not quite. JSX text is rendered as written.
~A compile error about an undefined name#Not quite. As plain text it is never evaluated, so no name is looked up.
=The literal text qty * 2#Correct. Text outside braces is literal. Only braces switch back to JavaScript.
}
